<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Anthem · 1897 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn written in 1897 by a South African teacher that became a song of freedom and part of several African national anthems.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Enoch Sontonga (first stanza and chorus); Samuel Mqhayi (seven further Xhosa stanzas, 1927)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Enoch Sontonga</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Xhosa Christians; the African National Congress and southern African liberation movements</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xhosa, Xitsonga, Sesotho, Swahili, Shona, Ndebele</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>a Methodist mission school near Johannesburg, South Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1897</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (first stanza and chorus by Enoch Sontonga (1897; he died in 1905); further stanzas by Samuel Mqhayi (1927))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a>&quot; (&quot;Lord Bless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>&quot;) was composed in 1897 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enoch-sontonga">Enoch Sontonga</a>, a Xhosa teacher at a Methodist mission school near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. He wrote it as a hymn of a single stanza in Xhosa, in B-flat major, with four-part harmony carrying a repetitive melody that draws on both Western hymn writing and South African melody. It was first sung in public at the ordination of John Hlengani Mboweni as a pastor in 1899, and Mboweni made the first translation, into Xitsonga, the same year. In 1927 the poet Samuel Mqhayi added seven more Xhosa stanzas, and the hymn was first published that year.</p><p>The choir of Ohlange High School, whose co-founder served as the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">South African Native National Congress</a>, took the hymn up, and the Rev. John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langalibalele">Langalibalele</a> Dube's Ohlange <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> Choir popularised it at concerts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. It was sung to close the Congress's meeting in 1912, and by 1925 it was the official closing anthem of the organisation, later the African National Congress (ANC). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Plaatje, a founding member of the ANC, had it recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1923, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> Mphahlele published a Sotho version in 1942. It spread as a church hymn and as an anthem of political meetings, and independence movements across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> sang it, including in Chichewa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p><p>Under apartheid the hymn was the ANC's official anthem and a symbol of the anti-apartheid movement; many regarded it as the unofficial national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, standing for the suffering of the oppressed majority. Because of its link to the ANC, the government banned it. During the 1970s and 1980s it was sung regularly at the meetings of the worldwide Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> Movement, which made it known far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The anthropologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Coplan wrote that it came to symbolise, more than any other piece of expressive culture, the struggle for African unity and liberation in South Africa.</p><p>Several states adopted it at independence. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> translation, &quot;Mungu ibariki Afrika&quot;, was first officially performed on 8 December 1961 in the new national stadium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, before 75,000 people, as Tanganyika's independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> approached: near midnight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Jack</a> was lowered to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot; for the last time, and the new flag was raised to the hymn's tune. It remained the anthem after Tanganyika's union with Zanzibar in 1964 formed Tanzania. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> used the hymn as its anthem from independence in 1964 until 1973, when new words, &quot;Stand and Sing of Zambia, Proud and Free&quot;, replaced the old ones on the same melody. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> adopted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> version, &quot;Ishe Komborera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>&quot;, at independence in 1980 and replaced it in 1994; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> used the hymn at independence in March 1990 and replaced it in 1991 with &quot;Namibia, Land of the Brave&quot;.</p><p>In 1994, after the end of apartheid, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> declared both &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a>&quot; and the former anthem, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a>&quot;, national anthems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the two were played together. In 1996 a shortened combination of the two was released as the new national anthem under the constitution, and it was adopted in 1997. It opens with lines in Xhosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, continues in Sesotho, then uses a section of &quot;Die Stem&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> and closes in English. Its official name is &quot;The National Anthem of South Africa&quot;, although it is often called &quot;Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika&quot;. The original melody is still used in the anthems of Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer asking God to bless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, written in the form and language of a church hymn. Sung in parts by a whole gathering, it came to stand for unity and for the hope of liberation as much as for its religious words.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Xhosa</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Xhosa</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Nkosi, sikelel' iAfrika</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord, bless Africa</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Malupakam' upondo lwayo;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May its horn be raised up;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Yiva imithandazo yethu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear our prayers</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Yihla Moya, yihla Moya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come down, Spirit, come down, Spirit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Yihla Moya Oyingcwele</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come down, Holy Spirit</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelela iNkosi zethu;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless our chiefs;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Zimkhumbule umDali wazo;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May they remember their Creator;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Zimoyike zezimhlonele,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May they fear him and revere him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Azisikelele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So that he may bless them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelel' amadol' esizwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless the men [?] of the nation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelela kwa nomlisela</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless the young man too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Ulithwal' ilizwe ngomonde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who carries the land with patience,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Uwusikilele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless it [?].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelel' amakosikazi;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless the married women;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Nawo onk'amanenekazi;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And all the ladies too;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Pakamisa wonk'umtinjana</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Raise up every young girl</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Uwusikilele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless her [?].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelela abafundisi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless the ministers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Bemvaba zonke zelilizwe;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of all the churches of this land;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Ubathwese ngoMoya Wako</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Clothe them with your Spirit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Ubasikelele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelel' ulimo nemfuyo;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless the farming and the livestock;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Gxotha zonk' iindlala nezifo;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Drive out all famines and diseases;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Zalisa ilizwe nempilo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fill the land with health</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Ulisikelele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless it.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sikelel' amalinga ethu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless our efforts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Awomanyana nokuzaka,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of coming together and of building ourselves up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Awemfundo nemvisiswano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of learning and of understanding one another</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Uwasikele</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless them</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Nkosi Sikelel, Afrika;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord, bless Africa;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Cima bonk' ubugwenxa bayo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Blot out all its wickedness</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Neziggito, Nezono zayo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And its transgressions [?], and its sins</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Uwasikelele.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bless them.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Enoch Sontonga (first stanza, 1897) and S. E. K. Mqhayi (further stanzas, 1927); the historic Xhosa text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nkosi_Sikelel'_iAfrika" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (12)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'upondo' is a horn; 'may its horn be raised' is the biblical image of strength and glory (as in the Psalms), not a musical horn.</li><li>line 2.1-2: 'Yihla' is 'come down, descend'; the stanza is a prayer to the Holy Spirit, as in Sontonga's hymn.</li><li>line 4.1: 'amadol'' read as 'amadolo', knees, used of those who hold the nation up (its elders or leaders); it could also be a misprint for 'amadoda', men. Unsure: please check against a printed Mqhayi text.</li><li>line 4.4, 5.4: 'Uwusikilele' carries an object concord (wu-) that does not match 'umlisela' or 'umtinjana' as nouns of class 1; translated loosely as 'it' / 'her'. Also note the spelling 'Uwusikilele' (4.4) beside 'Uwusikelele' (5.4) in the source.</li><li>line 5.1: 'amakosikazi' (modern amakhosikazi) can mean chiefs' wives, queens, or married women generally; rendered 'married women'.</li><li>line 5.3: 'umtinjana' (modern umthinjana) taken as 'young girl'.</li><li>line 6.2: 'imvaba' is a congregation or denomination; 'of all the churches' renders 'zonke iimvaba'.</li><li>line 8.2: 'ukuzaka' read as 'ukuzakha', to build oneself up (self-improvement); 'umanyano' is union, coming together.</li><li>line 8.4: the source has 'Uwasikele', apparently cut short for 'Uwasikelele'; translated as the other stanzas' last lines.</li><li>line 9.1: the source has 'Nkosi Sikelel, Afrika' with a stray comma; translated as stanza 1's first line.</li><li>line 9.3: 'Neziggito' looks like a misprint, probably for 'nezigqitho' (transgressions, trespasses); marked [?].</li><li>Spelling: the source keeps the older orthography (p for ph, t for th, k for kh: 'Malupakam'', 'umtinjana', 'amakosikazi'); left as it stands.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="5M8426dDv6A" data-title="Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika: Stellenbosch University Choir"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Stellenbosch University Choir</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Stellenbosch University Choir. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5M8426dDv6A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The National Anthem of South Africa</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1997</span><span class="sg-chain-h">combined with &quot;Die Stem van Suid-Afrika&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mungu ibariki Afrika (national anthem of Tanganyika, then Tanzania)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Swahili translation with partly modified words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ishe Komborera Africa (national anthem of Zimbabwe)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Shona and Ndebele version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stand and Sing of Zambia, Proud and Free (national anthem of Zambia)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1973</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">National anthem of Namibia</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Xitsonga translation by John Hlengani Mboweni</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1899</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sotho version by Moses Mphahlele</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cry Freedom (film; London Symphony Orchestra under André Previn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kuule, Isä taivaan, pyyntö tää (children's psalm, words by Jaakko Löytty)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Seëngebed (Elvis Blue and Coenie de Villiers)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Afrikaans translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Boom Shaka's kwaito version</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mungu Ibariki Mang'u High (school anthem, Kenya)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li></ul></section>
